from helper import *
b='content/os/06-file-systems/fs-modern-distributed';m=b+'.md';q=b+'.questions.json'
x=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/xfs_growfs.8.html'];z=['https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/man/master/7/zpoolconcepts.7.html','https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/man/master/7/zfsprops.7.html'];n=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/nfs.5.html','https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1813'];nf=['https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8881'];h=['https://hadoop.apache.org/docs/stable/hadoop-project-dist/hadoop-hdfs/HdfsDesign.html'];ec=['https://hadoop.apache.org/docs/stable/hadoop-project-dist/hadoop-hdfs/HDFSErasureCoding.html'];g=['https://storage.googleapis.com/gweb-research2023-media/pubtools/4446.pdf'];l=['https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-lfs.pdf']
def f(i,k,c,reason,s,o=None):qe(q,'fs-modern-distributed-q'+str(i),k,c,reason,s,o)
f(1,'prompt','Which filesystem has especially restricted, version-dependent shrink support: the current upstream manual describes shrinking only its last allocation group without removing that group?','Replace obsolete no-shrink premise.',x)
f(1,'explanation','Correct. Current upstream XFS tooling documents this limited operation, with additional single-allocation-group restrictions. It is not general arbitrary shrinking; installed kernel/tooling support and geometry matter.','Latest documented support.',x,2)
f(1,'explanation','False. ext4 shrinking requires an unmounted filesystem, and XFS has restricted, version-dependent shrink support.','Remove false no-shrink distractor explanation.',x,3)
f(1,'workedExample','1. ext4 supports online growth and offline shrinking.\n2. Btrfs supports online resizing, subject to space and layout constraints.\n3. XFS has historically lacked general shrink support. The current upstream `xfs_growfs` manual describes shrinking only the last allocation group without removing it; it also excludes shrinking a one-group filesystem or reducing one to a single group.\n4. Check the installed versions and exact geometry before making a resize plan. Do not treat this limited capability as arbitrary volume shrinking.','Replace stale capability table.',x)
f(1,'link',{'title':'xfs_growfs(8) — upstream manual','url':x[0]},'Direct capability source.',x)
f(2,'workedExample','1. In ext4, for inode N and I inodes per group, group = (N − 1) // I and index = (N − 1) % I. Its byte offset within that group\'s inode table is index × inode_size.\n2. LFS writes changed inodes to new log locations instead.\n3. The inode map records the current location of each inode.\n4. Imap pieces are logged too, and checkpoint information provides the recovery starting point.','Fix off-by-one and missing group arithmetic.',['https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/ext4/inodes.html']+l)
f(3,'prompt','An HDFS client reads a 10 GiB replicated file with 128 MiB blocks. How does the data travel?','Consistent binary units.',h)
f(3,'text','All 10 GiB stream through the NameNode, which fetches blocks from DataNodes','Keep option unit aligned.',h,0)
f(3,'explanation','There is no broadcast in this path. The NameNode maintains reported replica locations; failures can make those reports stale, so clients may need another replica.','Location knowledge not infallible.',h,1)
f(3,'workedExample','1. 10 GiB / 128 MiB = 80 blocks, numbered 0–79.\n2. The client obtains block locations from the NameNode, potentially in batches.\n3. It reads payload directly from suitable DataNodes, preferring nearby replicas and retrying alternatives when necessary.\n4. The NameNode handles metadata RPCs; DataNodes deliver the 10 GiB payload.','Units and fallback scope.',h)
f(4,'explanation','Correct. A\'s dirty data may still be local. With close-to-open enabled, successful writeback and no intervening writer, B\'s later open revalidates A\'s changes. Check write, fsync and close errors.','Successful operations and no later overwrite required.',n,1)
f(4,'workedExample','One possible execution is:\n\n1. A writes into its local dirty cache.\n2. A has not closed or fsynced; background writeback has not sent these bytes yet.\n3. B opens and revalidates against the server, which still has the old data.\n4. B reads old contents. Other timings can expose the new data sooner.\n\nApplications coordinating through close-to-open must successfully flush/close before signalling the reader, check errors, and account for other concurrent writers.','Previously presented possible trace as necessary.',n)
f(5,'prompt','For an explicitly simplified arithmetic model, assume **150 bytes** per file object and per block object, ignoring all other heap costs. An HDFS namespace has **100 million nonempty files**, each using one block. What modeled heap cost results?','Unverified empirical rule replaced explicit assumption.',h)
f(5,'explanation','The NameNode keeps namespace and block-management metadata in memory. DataNodes also keep local block information and checksums, but that does not eliminate NameNode heap use.','DataNodes do maintain metadata.',h,3)
f(5,'workedExample','1. Model objects: 100 million file objects + 100 million block objects = 200 million.\n2. Assumed cost: 200,000,000 × 150 bytes = 30,000,000,000 bytes = 30 GB (decimal).\n3. Packing small payloads into fewer larger files reduces the modeled file/block object count. Actual memory also depends on directories, replicas, strings, JVM representation and Hadoop version.\n\nThe 150-byte figure is a teaching assumption. No measured heap profile is provided, so this is not a production sizing estimate.','Expose measurement absence and remove misleading fixed sizing.',h)
f(6,'prompt','An eligible writer DataNode on rack R1 writes a replicated HDFS block with replication factor 3. Assume the default policy, enough eligible nodes on R1 and another rack, and a healthy completed pipeline. Where are replicas placed and how many cross-rack payload hops are used?','Writer/topology/eligibility assumptions needed.',h)
f(7,'prompt','For ordinary file payload read from storage, which configured setups use filesystem data checksums to detect checksum mismatches and can repair them when a valid redundant copy exists? Assume checksums are enabled, and exclude malicious checksum collisions. Select all that apply.','No guarantee on cache hits, disabled checksums or all corruption.',z)
f(7,'text','ZFS on a mirror, with file-data checksums enabled','Configuration scope.',z,0)
f(7,'explanation','Yes. Protected reads are checked against their stored checksums; a valid mirror copy can allow recovery and repair.','Conditional detection/repair.',z,0)
f(7,'text','Btrfs with RAID 1 and checksummed file data','Configuration scope.',['https://btrfs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Status.html'],1)
f(7,'explanation','Yes. With data checksums enabled, Btrfs can detect a mismatch and repair from a valid redundant copy. NOCOW/nodatasum files are excluded here.','Exceptions explicit.',['https://btrfs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Status.html'],1)
f(7,'explanation','No end-to-end file-data checksum is supplied by this combination. Ordinary md RAID 5 reads do not verify a checksum against the expected file bytes; parity also supports reconstruction and scrubbing, not just replacement of failed disks.','md parity has more uses than rebuild.',['https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/md.html'],3)
f(7,'workedExample','1. Ask whether the filesystem stores and checks a checksum covering the actual payload.\n2. Configured ZFS and Btrfs data checksums do so. A valid redundant copy is needed for repair.\n3. ext4 metadata checksums alone do not protect mutable file payload.\n4. XFS metadata checksums plus md parity do not provide the same file-data integrity check. A parity scrub can expose inconsistency without necessarily identifying which member has the correct bytes.\n5. Checksums are not a mathematical guarantee against every corruption pattern, and a cache hit is not necessarily a new storage read.','Match revised integrity question.',z+['https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/md.html'])
f(8,'prompt','In a steady-state LFS cleaning model that ignores metadata writes, cleaned segments are **80% live**. Per segment of new payload, how many segments of total writes are needed, including copied live data but excluding reads?','Define model and measure.',l)
f(8,'workedExample','1. Cleaning one segment copies 0.8 segment and yields 0.2 segment of net capacity.\n2. Clean 1 / 0.2 = 5 segments to make room for one segment of new payload.\n3. Copying writes total 5 × 0.8 = 4 segments; add one new segment: total writes = 5.\n4. Thus write amplification is 1 / (1 − u) = 5 in this model. Cleaner reads are additional I/O, excluded from this write-only measure. Age and utilization both affect LFS cleaning policy.','Do not equate cold preference with unconditional policy.',l)
f(9,'prompt','An NFSv3 server executes `REMOVE(dirfh, "tmp.lock")`, but its success reply is lost. Assume the retry is re-executed without duplicate suppression, nobody recreates the name, and the client does not normalize the second error. What reply reaches the application?','Remove ambiguity about caches, client normalization and concurrency.',n)
f(9,'explanation','False. A repeated REMOVE can find that the name has already disappeared. Even repeated reads can return different bytes if another client modifies the file.','Idempotence does not freeze state.',n,0)
f(9,'explanation','Correct under the stated assumptions. A duplicate-request cache can instead recognize the retransmission using RPC/client identity and replay a remembered success. Such a cache has bounded lifetime and capacity.','RPC XID alone not global identity.',n,1)
f(9,'workedExample','1. First REMOVE deletes the name and returns success, but that reply is lost.\n2. A re-executed REMOVE finds no name and returns ENOENT in this scenario.\n3. A duplicate-request cache can replay the first reply rather than execute again; it must distinguish clients as well as requests.\n4. NFSv3 EXCLUSIVE CREATE has a verifier specifically supporting retransmission, so not every exclusive-create retry simply fails.\n5. NFSv4.1 sessions add replay controls within defined session/slot/cache rules; they are not an unbounded guarantee of exactly-once application effects.','Correct exclusive CREATE verifier and bound exactly-once claim.',n+nf)
f(10,'prompt','A Hadoop cluster stores **600 TB** of payload with 3× replication. Assume full RS-6-3 stripes and ignore metadata, padding and other overhead. What modeled raw payload capacity and per-group erasure tolerance follow after conversion?','Ideal model not exact physical sizing.',ec)
f(10,'workedExample','1. Replication stores 600 × 3 = 1,800 TB of payload copies. Each block remains available with two lost replicas if its third survives.\n2. RS-6-3 stores nine cells for six data cells: 600 × 9 / 6 = 900 TB in this full-stripe model.\n3. Any six of a stripe\'s nine cells suffice; three missing cells are recoverable. This is a per-stripe condition, not a promise about arbitrary rack topology.\n4. Access to missing data requires reconstruction reads and CPU. Healthy cells can still be read directly; not every read after a node failure requires decoding.\n5. Short files and metadata affect actual storage use. The cited Hadoop 3.3.5 documentation also restricts append/truncate and sync behavior for striped files.','Failure/read scope and feature tradeoff.',ec)
f(11,'explanation','False. Exactly-once and producer invocation order are not promised. The primary does assign a consistent mutation order for its chunk, which differs from ordering by when distributed producers called append.','Original denied all ordering despite primary mutation order.',g,3)
f(11,'workedExample','1. The primary chooses an append offset and coordinates secondary mutations.\n2. A failure can cause the client to retry the record, potentially at a later offset.\n3. A replica where the first attempt succeeded may then contain a duplicate; other regions can contain padding or inconsistent bytes.\n\n```\nreplica A: R1 R2 R2 R3\nreplica B: R1 ?? R2 R3\n```\n\n4. This is an illustrative layout, not a trace from a live cluster. Applications use record checksums and unique IDs to skip invalid regions and deduplicate.','Retry layout not universal result.',g)
f(11,'link',{'title':'The Google File System — SOSP 2003 (PDF)','url':g[0]},'Working official PDF.',g)
f(12,'prompt','In a hypothetical ZFS RAID-Z2 HDD pool serving VMs over NFS, tracing identifies durable ZIL commit I/O as the synchronous-write bottleneck. Which change directly targets that bottleneck while retaining durability, assuming compatible devices and sufficient main-pool throughput?','Need evidence of bottleneck, no unsupported benchmark.',z)
f(12,'explanation','L2ARC caches reads; it does not directly move the durable ZIL commit path identified as the bottleneck.','Avoid claiming no indirect effects.',z,0)
f(12,'explanation','Correct as a candidate to validate. A suitably low-latency, durable SLOG can accelerate ZIL commits. Normal transaction-group writes still place the data in the main pool; no universal sub-millisecond latency follows.','Remove unsupported guaranteed latency.',z,2)
f(12,'explanation','Deduplication changes storage and lookup work depending on duplicate content; it does not directly replace the slow durable log device identified here.','No universal dedup performance verdict.',z,3)
f(12,'workedExample','1. A successful synchronous operation must satisfy the configured durability contract.\n2. If ZIL commit I/O on the HDD pool is the bottleneck, a separate suitable log device can shorten that path.\n3. Logged operations are later incorporated into normal main-pool transaction groups. The SLOG is a log, not a general write-back cache that permanently stores the dataset.\n4. The device must honor persistence requests. Power-loss protection can support that with low latency; mirroring protects against loss of a single log device. Log replay is needed after an unclean interruption when committed log operations have not reached their normal main-pool form.\n5. Measure the actual result: no identified device or reproducible benchmark supports fixed microsecond/millisecond guarantees here. `sync=disabled` weakens the contract rather than fixing durable commit performance.','Correct SLOG role, durability condition and evidence absence.',z)
